¶ These new notes have also introduced two new Semitones of 92 cents, *F* 92 *F₁*♯ and *D¹*♭ 92 *D*. But the smallest interval between any two notes remains the small Semitone of 70 cents, *A¹*♭ 70 *A₁*, *E¹*♭ 70 *E₁*, *B¹*♭ 70 *B₁*.

12. *Modulation into the Dominant Duodene.*

@@F@@
>>>
<<<
@@F@@
===
It is obvious from the construction of the duodene that the transition from any duodene to an adjacent one is very easy. Suppose (see scheme in art. 11) that we omit the lowest line *D¹*♭ + *F* + *A₁* (or *Ro* + *Fa* + *La*) and take in the line *F¹* + *A* + *C₁*♯ at the top, @@F@@
>>>
<<<
@@F@@
===
we shall have the duodene of *G*, which has three lines in common with that of *C*. The three new notes introduce 2 *commas* *F* 22 *F¹*, *A₁* 22 *A* and one *diaskhisma* *C₁*♯ 20 *D¹*♭. These minute distinctions neglected in tempered music have, @@F@@
>>>
<<<
@@F@@
===
however, a powerful effect on the harmony of justly intoned instruments. The *C₁*♯ is indeed one of the extra notes and does not occur in the decad of *G* or its scales. On the other hand *F₁*♯, which was an extra note in the duodene of *C*, becomes a substantive note @@F@@
>>>
<<<
@@F@@
===
in the decad, as well as duodene, of *G*, and we find then that the *F* of the *C* decad becomes the *F₁*♯, 92 cents higher in the *G* decad. This difference is so large that it cannot be disregarded in tempered music, and it is, accordingly, @@F@@
>>>
<<<
@@F@@
===
there represented by an interval of 100 cents, and forms the distinguishing mark for major scales of what is termed the *modulation into the dominant* as just explained.
